{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-34984" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The matrix $A$ and vectors ${b}_{1}$, ${b}_{2}$, ${b}_{3}$ are given below. Which of the vectors lie in $\\text{Null}(A)?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064430/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05b52825909934a0512cdab552abffb2799d70e561f1fb95b21200ec238a597"} {"question" "q-34984" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A)$ if $Ab=0.$ So, we need to compute the product of $A$ and each of the given vectors and check whether it is equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{b}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\\,✓ \\\\ A{b}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-4\\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ A{b}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=4\\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only ${b}_{1}$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064430/state.json" "file_sha256" "d05b52825909934a0512cdab552abffb2799d70e561f1fb95b21200ec238a597"} {"question" "q-34984" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064430/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "957f63cde2a2b80b6a3155a15a71a6f9a44b9558ecc0cc90896f8184b7336d1a"} {"question" "q-34984" "field" "selection" "attribute"