)$."] [17592186120299 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -15 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$. Find $Null(A)$."] [17592186120299 :question/answer-fields 17592186118269] [17592186120257 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 12\\end{bmatrix}$. Find $Null(A)$."] [17592186120257 :question/answer-fields 17592186118042] [17592186119550 :question/problem "The matrix $A$ and vectors $\\mathbf{b}_1$, $\\mathbf{b}_2$, $\\mathbf{b}_3$ are given below. Which of the vectors lie in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ -5 & 10 \\\\ -8 & 16\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186119550 :question/worked-solution "A vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ if\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to compute the product of $A$ and each of the given vectors and check whether it is equal to $\\mathbf{0}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAb_{1} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ -5 & 10 \\\\ -8 & 16\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-27 \\\\ 45 \\\\ 72\\end{bmatrix} \\ne 0\\cdot \\\\\nAb_{2} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ -5 & 10 \\\\ -8 & 16\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = 0✓ \\\\\nAb_{3} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ -5 & 10 \\\\ -8 & 16\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 40\\end{bmatrix} \\ne 0\\cdot\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $\\mathbf{b}_2$ lies in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$."] [17592186119551 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ k \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ Find the value of $k$ such that $\\mathbf{b}\\in\\textrm{Null}(A)$."] [17592186119551 :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Null}(A)$, then we must have\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAb &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ k \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 + 2k - 12 \\\\ -6 + 3k - 3 \\\\ 12 - 6k + 6\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{2k - 6 = 0; 3k - 9 = 0; - 6k + 18 = 0\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=3$."] [17592186119552 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -12 \\\\ 3 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$ Find $\\textrm{Null}(A)$."] [17592186119552 :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -12 \\\\ 3 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1} &:=\\frac{1}{4}R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -8\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{2} + (-3)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{x_{1} - 3x_{2} = 0; x_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nThe second equation tells us that $x_2=0$. Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} - 3x_{2} &= 0 \\\\\nx_{1} - 3(0) &= 0 \\\\\nx_{1} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is the only solution to this system.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\left\\{\\mathbf{0} \\right\\}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186118043 17592186118044 17592186118045 17592186118046 17592186118047 17592186118264 17592186118265 17592186118266 17592186118267 17592186118268 17592186118270 17592186118271 17592186118272 17592186118273 17592186118274] "immutable_fields" [[17592186118042 :answer-field/correct] [17592186118042 :answer-field/id] [17592186118042 :answer-field/key] [17592186118042 :answer-field/type] [17592186118263 :answer-fiel